**Theorem 4.2 (The interior determinant is indispensable).** The functional
$\Phi$ is nonnegative on $\mathcal K_\partial$, but is negative on
some full-sector real product atoms. As an instance of (3.6), its
coefficients satisfy all fifteen sign conditions and all four scalar
inequalities of Theorem 3.2, while its cubic is strictly negative
at every strictly positive population.

Proof. For arbitrary real $a,b$, let

$$
p_i=\|R_ia\|^2,\quad q_i=\|R_ib\|^2,\quad
x_0=(d^Ta)(d^Tb),\quad x_+=(R_+a)^T(R_+b),\quad
x_-=(R_-a)^T(R_-b).
\tag{4.2}
$$

The normalized basis (1.6) gives
$n_iM_{ii}(a,b)=p_iq_i$,
$\eta_+(a,b)=x_0x_+$, $\eta_-(a,b)=x_0x_-$, and
$\eta_\Delta(a,b)+\eta_\Sigma(a,b)=x_+x_-$.
For the last identity write the plane coordinates as
$a_+=(a_1,a_2)$, $a_-=(a_3,a_4)$ and similarly for $b$.
The two coordinate pairs in (1.6) are
$(a_1a_3+a_2a_4,a_2a_3-a_1a_4)$ and
$(a_1a_3-a_2a_4,a_2a_3+a_1a_4)$.
Half the sum of their pairings with the corresponding $b$ pairs
is exactly $(a_1b_1+a_2b_2)(a_3b_3+a_4b_4)$.
Consequently

$$
\Phi(k(a,b))=\sum_ip_iq_i
          -\frac32(x_0x_++x_0x_-+x_+x_-),\qquad
x_i^2\le p_iq_i.
\tag{4.3}
$$

If either factor misses sector $i$, both $p_iq_i$ and $x_i$
vanish. For the other two sectors $j,k$,

$$
\Phi(k(a,b))\ge x_j^2+x_k^2-\frac32x_jx_k
\ge\frac14(x_j^2+x_k^2)\ge0.
\tag{4.4}
$$

The middle inequality follows from
$x_jx_k\le(x_j^2+x_k^2)/2$, so arbitrary signs are included.
If further sectors vanish, the same argument still applies.
Nonnegative sums prove the assertion on $\mathcal K_\partial$.

For $a_*=b_*=(d+u_++u_-)/\sqrt3$, all $p_i,q_i,x_i$ equal
$1/3$. Equation (4.3) gives
$\Phi(k(a_*,b_*))=1/3-1/2=-1/6$.

To see precisely the omitted determinant, take in (3.1)

$$
C=-\operatorname{diag}(1,2,2),\quad y=0,\quad
\delta_+=\delta_-=\delta_\Delta=\delta_\Sigma=3/2,
\quad \delta_{2+}=\delta_{2-}=0.
\tag{4.5}
$$

Then $b^T\mathcal B(a)b=\Phi(k(a,b))$. At a canonical preparation,

$$
\begin{gathered}
\mathcal R(p)=\operatorname{diag}(\sqrt p)\,
\begin{pmatrix}1&-3/4&-3/4\\-3/4&1&-3/4\\-3/4&-3/4&1\end{pmatrix}
\operatorname{diag}(\sqrt p),\qquad
\mathcal T(p)=\operatorname{diag}(p_+,p_-),\\
\det\mathcal R(p)=-\frac{49}{32}p_0p_+p_-.
\end{gathered}
\tag{4.6}
$$

The coefficient signs are zero or one. Each radial scalar inequality
is $3/4\le1$, and the tangent one is $0\le1$.
Each radial pair minor equals $7p_ip_j/16$. The constant matrix
in (4.6) has eigenvalues $-1/2,7/4,7/4$, proving its determinant
$-49/32$. Thus its interior cubic is negative despite every finite
condition passing. This is a general invariant-functional example;
no particular positive event-multiplier realization of (4.5) is
asserted. $\square$

